--- [number_theory.valuation.lte] ---
Triggers: x^n-y^n or x^n+y^n, highest prime power dividing, p does not divide x or y
Technique: Lifting the Exponent Lemma (LTE): For odd prime p with p|a-b, p∤a, p∤b: ν_p(a^n-b^n) = ν_p(a-b) + ν_p(n). For a^n+b^n with odd n: ν_p(a^n+b^n) = ν_p(a+b) + ν_p(n). CASE p=2: ν_2(a^n-b^n) = ν_2(a-b) + ν_2(a+b) + ν_2(n) - 1 when 2|a-b and n even. For odd n: ν_2(a^n-b^n) = ν_2(a-b). TRAP: LTE requires THREE conditions: (1) p|a-b, (2) p∤a and p∤b, (3) for p=2, use the separate formula. If you apply LTE without checking ALL conditions, you get plausible but WRONG results. If conditions fail, work out the valuation by hand.
Question: When a problem asks about v_p(a^n - b^n) or involves Diophantine equations with prime power divisibility like "find all n such that p | a^n - b^n", or proving x^p + y^p = z^q has no solutions.
Answer: When to use LTE: (1) You need v_p(a^n ± b^n) and p divides a∓b. (2) Diophantine equations involving prime powers. (3) Proving divisibility of expressions like a^n - b^n by specific primes. Key conditions: for v_p(a^n - b^n), need p | a-b AND p ∤ a, p ∤ b. For the '+' version v_p(a^n + b^n), need p | a+b AND n odd. The lemma reduces complicated valuations to simple sums. Common trap: forgetting the p=2 special case which has extra v_2(a+b) term. Combine with Fermat's Little Theorem and order arguments for full solutions.

